AWS ECR — Detailed Interview Explanation

ECR = Amazon Elastic Container Registry

ECR is an AWS-managed container image registry. It is used to store, manage, and retrieve Docker/container images.

Think of it as:

Docker Hub, but hosted and integrated with AWS.

1. Why do we need ECR?

Suppose you have a Java/Python/Node.js application.

You create a Docker image:

Application Code
      ↓
Dockerfile
      ↓
docker build
      ↓
Docker Image

ECS needs that Docker image to start your container.

So we store the image in ECR:

Developer
   ↓
Docker build
   ↓
Docker Image
   ↓
ECR
   ↓
ECS
   ↓
Container
2. ECR Repository

Inside ECR, you create a repository.

For example:

ECR
│
└── my-app
      │
      ├── v1
      ├── v2
      └── latest

my-app is the repository.

The different versions are image tags.

For example:

my-app:v1
my-app:v2
my-app:latest
3. Real-world example

Suppose you are developing an application called:

shopping-app

You have:

Dockerfile
app.py
requirements.txt

You build the image:

docker build -t shopping-app .

Now locally:

Docker Images
└── shopping-app:latest

But ECS cannot use your local image.

So you push it to ECR.

4. Create ECR repository

Create:

Repository name:
shopping-app

ECR gives you a repository URI similar to:

123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/shopping-app

Your image will look like:

123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/shopping-app:latest
5. Login to ECR

Before pushing, Docker needs to authenticate with ECR.

Typically:

aws ecr get-login-password --region ap-south-1 \
| docker login \
--username AWS \
--password-stdin 123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com

After successful login:

Docker
   ↓
Authenticated
   ↓
ECR
6. Tag the image

Your local image is:

shopping-app:latest

You need to give it the ECR repository address:

docker tag shopping-app:latest \
123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/shopping-app:latest

Now:

Local Docker Image

shopping-app:latest

        ↓ tag

123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/
shopping-app:latest

Tagging does not upload the image.

It only gives the image the repository name.

7. Push image to ECR

Now:

docker push \
123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/shopping-app:latest

Flow:

Developer Laptop
       |
       | docker push
       ↓
      ECR
       |
       └── shopping-app
             └── latest

Now the image is stored in AWS.

8. How ECS uses ECR

Now comes the important part.

You create an ECS Task Definition.

Inside the Task Definition, you specify:

Container image:

123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/
shopping-app:latest

Then:

ECS Service
     ↓
Task
     ↓
Pull image from ECR
     ↓
Docker Container
     ↓
Shopping Application

So ECR doesn't run the container.

ECR only stores/provides the image.

ECS runs the container.

9. Complete example

Imagine you're deploying a Node.js application.

Step 1 — Developer writes code
Node.js Application
Step 2 — Dockerfile
FROM node:20

WORKDIR /app

COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm install

COPY . .

EXPOSE 3000

CMD ["npm", "start"]
Step 3 — Build
docker build -t shopping-app:v1 .
Step 4 — Login to ECR
aws ecr get-login-password --region ap-south-1 |
docker login --username AWS --password-stdin \
123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com
Step 5 — Tag
docker tag shopping-app:v1 \
123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/shopping-app:v1
Step 6 — Push
docker push \
123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/shopping-app:v1

Now:

ECR Repository
└── shopping-app
      └── v1
Step 7 — ECS Task Definition
Container Name:
shopping-container

Image:
123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/shopping-app:v1

Port:
3000
Step 8 — ECS Service

Suppose:

Desired Tasks = 3

ECS creates:

ECS Service
    |
    ├── Task 1 → shopping-app:v1
    ├── Task 2 → shopping-app:v1
    └── Task 3 → shopping-app:v1

Each task pulls the image from ECR.

10. What happens when we push a new version?

Suppose you modify your application.

You create:

shopping-app:v2

Then:

docker build -t shopping-app:v2 .

Tag:

docker tag shopping-app:v2 \
123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/shopping-app:v2

Push:

docker push \
123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/shopping-app:v2

Now ECR contains:

shopping-app
│
├── v1
└── v2

You update the ECS Task Definition:

v1 → v2

ECS then starts new tasks using:

shopping-app:v2
11. ECR and latest

You will often see:

my-app:latest

latest is just a tag.

It does not necessarily mean "newest image automatically running everywhere."

For production, it's generally better to use a unique tag such as:

my-app:v1
my-app:v2
my-app:2026-08-29
my-app:git-abc123

This makes deployments easier to identify and roll back.

12. ECR Public vs Private

ECR has two major repository types.

Private ECR

Used for your organization's private application images.

Your AWS Account
      ↓
Private ECR
      ↓
Private Docker Images
Public ECR

Used when you want container images to be publicly available.

For most company applications:

Private ECR

is used.

13. ECR permissions — important interview question

The user/developer needs permission to push images.

For example, IAM permissions can allow actions such as:

ecr:GetAuthorizationToken
ecr:BatchCheckLayerAvailability
ecr:InitiateLayerUpload
ecr:UploadLayerPart
ecr:CompleteLayerUpload
ecr:PutImage

For ECS pulling from ECR, the task execution role commonly provides permissions needed to retrieve the image.

This is important:

Developer
   ↓
IAM User/Role
   ↓
ECR
   ↓
Push Image

And:

ECS Task
   ↓
Task Execution Role
   ↓
ECR
   ↓
Pull Image
14. ECR image scanning

ECR can scan container images for known security vulnerabilities.

For example:

ECR
 ↓
Image
 ↓
Vulnerability Scan
 ↓
CVE findings

You might see findings such as:

Critical
High
Medium
Low

This helps identify vulnerable packages in your container image.

15. ECR lifecycle policy

Suppose developers push hundreds of images:

v1
v2
v3
...
v500

Keeping every old image forever may unnecessarily increase storage.

You can configure a lifecycle policy.

Example:

Keep latest 10 images
Delete older images

So ECR automatically cleans up old images according to your rules.

16. ECR image layers

Docker images are made of layers.

For example:

Docker Image
     |
     ├── Base OS layer
     ├── Runtime layer
     ├── Dependencies
     └── Application code

When pushing images, Docker/ECR can reuse layers that already exist.

For example:

v1
 ├── Node layer
 ├── npm dependencies
 └── App code

v2
 ├── Node layer       ← already exists
 ├── npm dependencies ← already exists
 └── New app code     ← changed

Only the necessary new layer data needs to be uploaded.

