The ALB is not under the containers. The direction in the earlier diagram was reversed for traffic.

For a web application, the correct traffic flow is:

                 Users
                   ↓
                  ALB
                   ↓
            ECS Service
                   ↓
        ┌──────────┼──────────┐
        ↓          ↓          ↓
      Task 1     Task 2     Task 3
        ↓          ↓          ↓
   Container   Container   Container
    App A        App A       App A
How ALB connects to ECS

Suppose your application runs on port 8080 inside the containers.

You create:

ALB
 ↓
Target Group
 ↓
ECS Tasks

The target group contains the running ECS task IPs/ports:

Target Group
 ├── 10.0.1.10:8080 → Task 1
 ├── 10.0.2.10:8080 → Task 2
 └── 10.0.3.10:8080 → Task 3

The ECS Service automatically registers/deregisters tasks with the target group when tasks start or stop.

Example request

A user opens:

https://myapp.com

Flow:

User
 ↓
ALB :443
 ↓
Target Group
 ↓
Task 2 :8080
 ↓
Container
 ↓
Application

Another request might go:

User
 ↓
ALB
 ↓
Target Group
 ↓
Task 1
 ↓
Container
Important relationship
Internet
   ↓
  ALB
   ↓
Target Group
   ↓
ECS Service
   ↓
Tasks
   ↓
Containers

ALB does not run inside the container.
ALB is a separate AWS managed service that sends traffic to your ECS containers.

Also, the ECS Service is what maintains the desired number of tasks; the ALB only distributes incoming traffic and performs health checks.




Yes ✅ ECS can use Docker Hub. It does not require ECR.

The key is that ECS needs a container image, and that image can come from different registries.

Option 1: ECS + ECR
Docker image
     ↓
    ECR
     ↓
    ECS
     ↓
Container

This is very common in AWS because ECR integrates directly with AWS IAM and ECS.

Option 2: ECS + Docker Hub
Docker image
     ↓
 Docker Hub
     ↓
    ECS
     ↓
Container

In the ECS Task Definition, you specify the Docker Hub image:

Image:
nginx:latest

ECS can pull that image from Docker Hub.

For a private Docker Hub repository, you need to configure the required credentials/secrets so ECS can authenticate.

Interview point

If asked:

"Is ECR mandatory for ECS?"

Answer:

No. ECS can pull container images from ECR, Docker Hub, or other supported container registries. ECR is commonly preferred for AWS workloads because it integrates with AWS IAM and other AWS services.

So remember:

ECS = runs the container
ECR = one possible place to get the image from
Docker Hub = another possible place to get the image from




ECS + ECR

When ECS pulls a private ECR image, it still needs permission.

Usually the ECS Task Execution Role has permissions such as:

ecr:GetAuthorizationToken
ecr:BatchGetImage
ecr:GetDownloadUrlForLayer
ecr:BatchCheckLayerAvailability

So:

ECS Task
   ↓
Task Execution Role
   ↓
Private ECR
   ↓
Pull image

You don't normally provide a Docker Hub username/password because AWS IAM handles access to ECR.

ECS + private Docker Hub

For a private Docker Hub repository, ECS needs Docker Hub credentials.

Typically:

ECS Task
   ↓
Secrets Manager
   ↓
Docker Hub credentials
   ↓
Docker Hub
   ↓
Pull image
ECS + public Docker Hub

If the image is public:

ECS
 ↓
Public Docker Hub
 ↓
Pull image

No private Docker Hub credentials are required, although Docker Hub rate limits can apply.

⭐ Interview answer

For private ECR images, ECS uses an IAM Task Execution Role to get permission to pull the image. For private external registries such as Docker Hub, ECS needs registry credentials, commonly stored securely in AWS Secrets Manager. Public images don't require authentication.

So ECR does require permission too — the difference is that ECR uses AWS IAM, while a private external registry generally uses registry credentials.




Yes ✅ If you mean a public ECR image, authentication to the ECR registry is generally not required to pull the public image.

Private ECR
ECS Task
   ↓
Task Execution Role
   ↓
Private ECR
   ↓
Pull image

IAM permissions are required.

Public ECR
ECS Task
   ↓
Public ECR
   ↓
