🚀 What is ECS?

ECS = Elastic Container Service

ECS is an AWS service used to run and manage Docker containers.

For example, suppose you have a web application:

Your Application
      ↓
Docker Image
      ↓
ECR
      ↓
ECS
      ↓
Docker Container running
      ↓
Users access application

So:

Docker → creates the container/image
ECR → stores the Docker image
ECS → runs and manages the container

Yes 👍 If you are talking about Fargate, AWS manages the underlying servers for you.

For example, if your ECS Service has:

Desired count = 2

You have:

Fargate
 ├── Task 1
 └── Task 2

If you increase it to:

Desired count = 5

ECS/Fargate will arrange the compute needed to run:

Fargate
 ├── Task 1
 ├── Task 2
 ├── Task 3
 ├── Task 4
 └── Task 5

You don't create or manage EC2 instances for those tasks.

So:

Fargate = AWS manages the underlying infrastructure; you manage the containers/tasks.

And if you configure ECS Service Auto Scaling, ECS can automatically increase or decrease the number of tasks based on CPU, memory, request count, or other metrics.


1. Main components of ECS

There are several important terms:

ECS
 │
 ├── Cluster
 │
 ├── Task Definition
 │
 ├── Task
 │
 ├── Service
 │
 └── Container

Let's understand each one.

2. ECS Cluster

A cluster is a logical grouping of your ECS resources.

Example:

ECS Cluster
   │
   ├── Task 1
   ├── Task 2
   └── Task 3

Suppose your application is called my-app.

You could create:

Cluster name:
my-app-cluster

Then ECS runs your application inside this cluster.

Important

A cluster itself does not necessarily mean an EC2 server.

ECS has two major ways to run containers:

ECS on Fargate

AWS manages the servers for you.

ECS
 ↓
Fargate
 ↓
Container
ECS on EC2

You manage the EC2 instances.

ECS
 ↓
EC2
 ↓
Docker Container

We'll come back to this.

3. Task Definition

This is very important.

A Task Definition tells ECS how to run your container.

Think of it as a configuration file/instruction manual.

For example:

Task Definition
       |
       ├── Container name
       ├── Docker image
       ├── CPU
       ├── Memory
       ├── Port
       ├── Environment variables
       └── IAM role

Example:

Task Definition: my-app-task

Container:
    Name: my-app

Image:
    123456789.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/my-app:latest

CPU:
    0.5 vCPU

Memory:
    1 GB

Port:
    80

This tells ECS:

"Run this Docker image with this amount of CPU/memory and expose port 80."

4. Task

A Task is a running instance of a Task Definition.

For example, you have:

Task Definition
my-app-task

You tell ECS to run it.

ECS creates:

Task
 └── Container
      └── my-app

If you run 3 tasks:

ECS Cluster
    │
    ├── Task 1 → Container
    ├── Task 2 → Container
    └── Task 3 → Container

So:

Task Definition = instructions

Task = running copy of those instructions

1. Most common: 1 Task → 1 Container

For a simple application:

Task
 └── Container
      └── Cart Application

For example:

Cart Task
   ↓
Cart Container

If your service needs 3 copies:

Cart Service
 ├── Task 1 → Cart Container
 ├── Task 2 → Cart Container
 └── Task 3 → Cart Container
2. One Task → Multiple Containers

You can also put multiple containers in the same Task:

Task
 ├── Container 1 → Main Application
 └── Container 2 → Sidecar

For example:

Task
 ├── Web App Container
 └── Logging Container
These containers are closely related and run together.

Task 1
├── Cart Container 1
└── Cart Container 2


Important distinction
Service
   ↓
Multiple Tasks
   ↓
Each Task can have
1 or more Containers

Example:

Cart Service
   |
   ├── Task 1
   │    ├── Cart Container
   │    └── Logging Container
   │
   └── Task 2
        ├── Cart Container
        └── Logging Container

So Task ≠ Container.

Task is the unit ECS runs. A Task contains one or more containers.

5. Service

This is another very important ECS concept.

An ECS Service keeps the required number of tasks running.

Suppose you want:

Desired count = 3

ECS will try to maintain:

Task 1 → Running
Task 2 → Running
Task 3 → Running

If Task 2 crashes:

Task 1 → Running
Task 2 → ❌
Task 3 → Running

ECS Service notices that only 2 are running.

It starts another:

Task 1 → Running
Task 2 → New Task
Task 3 → Running

Now again:

3 tasks running

That's one of the main purposes of an ECS Service.

6. Container

The actual Docker container runs inside the ECS task.

For example:

ECS Cluster
      ↓
   Service
      ↓
    Tasks
      ↓
 Containers

Example:

my-app-service
      |
      ├── Task 1
      |     └── my-app container
      |
      ├── Task 2
      |     └── my-app container
      |
      └── Task 3
            └── my-app container
7. Where does ECR come into this?

This is where ECR and ECS connect.

Suppose you build your application:

docker build -t my-app .

You get:

my-app Docker Image

You push it to ECR:

ECR
└── my-app
     └── latest

Then your ECS Task Definition says:

Image:
123456789.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/my-app:latest

When ECS starts the task:

ECS Task
   ↓
Pull image
   ↓
ECR
   ↓
Docker Image
   ↓
Container starts
