11. ECS Service + Load Balancer

For a web application, normally you don't want users directly accessing individual containers.

You can use an Application Load Balancer (ALB).

Example:

                 Users
                   |
                   ↓
                  ALB
                   |
          ┌────────┴────────┐
          ↓                 ↓
       Task 1             Task 2
       port 80            port 80
          |                 |
       Container         Container

The ALB distributes traffic.

For example:

User 1 → Task 1
User 2 → Task 2
User 3 → Task 1
User 4 → Task 2

If Task 1 becomes unhealthy, the ALB can stop sending traffic to it.

12. ECS networking

ECS tasks need networking.

With the common awsvpc network mode, a task gets an IP address inside your VPC.

For example:

VPC
 |
 ├── Public Subnet
 |
 └── Private Subnet
        |
        ├── ECS Task 1
        │     IP: 10.0.2.10
        |
        └── ECS Task 2
              IP: 10.0.2.11

A common production setup is:

Internet
   ↓
Internet Gateway
   ↓
ALB
   ↓
Private Subnet
   ↓
ECS Tasks

The ECS containers don't necessarily need public IP addresses.

13. Security Groups

You can use security groups to control traffic.

Example:

Internet
   ↓
ALB Security Group
   ↓ port 80/443
ECS Security Group
   ↓
Container

You could configure:

ALB Security Group
Allow:
80
443

from the internet.

ECS Security Group
Allow:
Port 80
Source = ALB Security Group

So users cannot directly access the ECS task.

14. ECS Deployment

Suppose you have version 1:

my-app:v1

You push it to ECR.

ECS runs:

Task 1 → v1
Task 2 → v1

Now you develop version 2:

my-app:v2

Push to ECR:

ECR
├── my-app:v1
└── my-app:v2

Update the ECS Task Definition to use:

my-app:v2

Then ECS Service performs a deployment.

For example:

Old:
Task 1 → v1
Task 2 → v1

Deployment

New:
Task 1 → v2
Task 2 → v2

ECS gradually replaces the old tasks depending on the deployment configuration.

