Task = running copy

Container = actual application process

And different applications do not automatically require different clusters.






Exactly — this is where Load Balancer + routing comes in.

Suppose Amazon has several services:

www.amazon.com
      ↓
Application Load Balancer (ALB)
      ↓
   ┌──┴──────────────────────────────┐
   ↓                                 ↓
/products                         /cart
   ↓                                 ↓
Product Service                  Cart Service
   ↓                                 ↓
Tasks                            Tasks
Example

When the user opens:

www.amazon.com/products

ALB looks at the URL path:

/products

and sends the request to the Product Service.

If the user opens:

www.amazon.com/cart

ALB sees:

/cart

and sends it to the Cart Service.

                    ALB
                     |
       ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
       ↓             ↓             ↓
 /products          /cart       /orders
       ↓             ↓             ↓
Product Service  Cart Service  Order Service
       ↓             ↓             ↓
    Tasks          Tasks          Tasks

So the user doesn't directly choose an ECS Service.

The user only accesses a URL such as:

www.amazon.com/cart

and the ALB routes the request to the correct ECS Service.

What if the applications are completely different?

For example:

amazon.com
hdfc.com
ola.com

You can use host-based routing:

                    ALB
                     |
        ┌────────────┼────────────┐
        ↓            ↓            ↓
 amazon.com      hdfc.com      ola.com
        ↓            ↓            ↓
 Amazon Service HDFC Service  Ola Service

So there are two common routing methods:

Path-based routing

amazon.com/cart       → Cart Service
amazon.com/products   → Product Service

Host-based routing

amazon.com → Amazon
hdfc.com   → HDFC
ola.com    → Ola

This is how users reach the particular application/service they need, while ECS handles the containers behind those services.






If you mean “Where is the ALB in the application architecture?”, it sits in front of the ECS Services.

For one application, for example an e-commerce application:

                    User
                     |
                     | https://amazon.com
                     ↓
                 Route 53
                     |
                     ↓
                  ALB
            (Application Load Balancer)
                     |
          ┌──────────┼──────────┐
          ↓          ↓          ↓
       /cart      /products   /orders
          ↓          ↓          ↓
     Cart Service Product     Order
                  Service     Service
          ↓          ↓          ↓
       ECS Tasks  ECS Tasks   ECS Tasks
Where is the ALB created?

The ALB is an AWS resource, separate from ECS.

You create it in your AWS environment:

AWS
 |
 ├── Route 53
 |
 ├── ALB
 |
 └── ECS
      └── Cluster
           ├── Cart Service
           ├── Product Service
           └── Order Service

Then you connect the ALB to ECS services using Target Groups.

ALB
 |
 ├── Listener Rule: /cart
 |       ↓
 |   Cart Target Group
 |       ↓
 |   Cart ECS Service
 |
 ├── Listener Rule: /products
 |       ↓
 |   Product Target Group
 |       ↓
 |   Product ECS Service
 |
 └── Listener Rule: /orders
         ↓
     Order Target Group
         ↓
     Order ECS Service

So ALB is not inside the container or inside the ECS Service. It is an AWS networking component in front of the ECS Services.

If the user types amazon.com/cart, the request goes:

User → Route 53 → ALB → Cart Target Group → Cart ECS Service → Cart Task → Container.







